course: dictionary
lesson: 3900
title: Friend: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word friend can be a noun or a verb. A friend is someone you like, who likes you, and who you enjoy being with. «Bullies pick on children who are alone more often, so staying near friends and adults helps.» Here friends means people who stand with you and keep you safe.
explain: The meaning can stretch further. «If you think a friend has stolen something, you have a duty to act, starting by talking to them or a trusted adult.» A friend can also be someone who helps or supports you. Read the words around friend to see which meaning fits.
explain: Friend can be a verb online. To friend someone is to add them to your list, and to unfriend is to remove them. Casual words like mate, buddy, pal and bestie also mean friend, but the exam voice says companion, peer or associate.
fact: Friend is a noun (friends) and a verb (to friend someone online, or to unfriend them).
fact: A friend is a person you like, who likes you, and who you enjoy being with.
fact: A friend can also be someone who helps or supports you, such as a friend of the planet.
fact: Mate (UK, Ireland, Australia, NZ), buddy and pal (US, Canada), and bestie or BFF are casual words for friend.
fact: In the exam voice, friend can be replaced by companion, associate, acquaintance, peer, or a fellow pupil.
exercise: «Bullies pick on children who are alone more often, so staying near friends and adults helps.» In this sentence, what does friends mean? | Things you own | Pictures of people | *People who stand with you and keep you safe | Numbers you add together
exercise: On a phone or tablet, what does it mean to friend someone? | To forget their name | *To add them to your list | To give them a present | To hit them with a ball
exercise: Which casual word means a very close friend? | Acquaintance | Associate | *Bestie | Companion
test: «A girl in Toronto saw that her friend looked pale and would not talk, so she told a teacher.» Why did she tell a teacher? | To get her friend into trouble | *Because her friend needed help | Because she was bored at lunch | To stop being friends
test: In the exam voice, which word can replace friend? | *Peer | Ladder | Puddle | Chore
exam: Which sentence uses friend as a verb? | My friends came round to play | *I will friend you on the app | My friend is a brilliant cook | Our friend lives next door
